# ~1-minute poll: an ops-request is an operator waiting on an answer,
# so the loop is minutes, not the estate observers' daily cadence.
# systemd will not start the oneshot while a previous run is still
# active, so overlapping polls cannot double-answer a packet.
#
# THIS TIMER IS THE NET, and it also brings up the waker (backlog
# ca6f40c2): boss-ops-runner-wake.service starts a pass the moment a
# request for this host is filed, so a request no longer waits out this
# timer's phase (median 33 s on the forge, 2026-10-18/19). Wants= starts
# it with the timer — at boot, and on every converge's `enable --now`,
# which also restarts a waker that died — and the waker is PartOf= this
# timer, so whatever stops the timer (retire-ops-runner's one act) stops
# it too. A host that installs no waker (one whose runner starts through
# a tree helper — see install-ops-runner.sh) has nothing for Wants= to
# find, which systemd skips. The cadence below is unchanged and owes the
# waker nothing; the stall alarm counts in it (RUNNER_CADENCE_S).
[Unit]
Description=BOSS ops-request runner poll (about every minute)
Wants=boss-ops-runner-wake.service

[Timer]
OnBootSec=2min
OnUnitActiveSec=1min
AccuracySec=15s

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
